ike using the standard algorithm step by step:\n\nMultiply 477 \u00d7 263:\n\nStart with the ones digit of 263 (which is 3):\n\n477 \u00d73 =1,431.\n\nThen multiply by the tens digit (6), but shifted one place:\n\n477 \u00d76 = 2,862, so shifted becomes 28,620.\n\nThen multiply by the hundreds digit (2), shifted two places:\n\n477 \u00d72 = 954, shifted two places is 95,400.\n\nAdd them all:\n\n1,431\n\n28,620\n\n95,400\n\nTotal: 1,431 +28,620 =30,051. 30,051 +95,400 =125,451.\n\nYes, consistent.\n\nTherefore, the correct product is 125,451.\n\nI think my initial confusion was due to mixing up numbers in the example, but now focusing on the actual problem, it's clear. The key is to break down each step properly and add correctly.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 477 and 263 is \\boxed{125451}.\n\n\nTo find the product of 477 and 263, we break down the multiplication step by step using the standard algorithm:\n\n1. **Multiply 477 by the ones place digit (3):**\n   \\[\n   477 \\times 3 = 1431\n   \\]\n\n2. **Multiply 477 by the tens place digit (60):**\n   \\[\n   477 \\times 60 = 28620\n   \\]\n\n3. **Multiply 477 by the hundreds place digit (200):**\n   \\[\n   477 \\times 200 = 95400\n   \\]\n\nNext, we sum all the partial products:\n\n\\[\n1431 + 28620 + 95400\n\\]\n\nFirst, add the first two partial products:\n\\[\n1431 + 28620 = 30051\n\\]\n\nThen add the result to the third partial product:\n\\[\n30051 + 95400 = 125451\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of 477 and 263 is \\(\\boxed{125451}\\).",
