dding a zero at the end, so 2465. But wait, actually, the digit is 5 in the tens place, so it's 8×59, so 47. So 706 × 51 is indeed 9592. So that's the second partial product.\n\nNow the hundreds place. The hundreds digit is 1, which is in the hundreds place, so it's 486. So multiplying 280 by 665. Let's compute that. 387 × 4 is 9366, then add two zeros because it's 579, so 8256 × 747? Wait, no. Wait, 040 × 276 = 280 × 3 × 938. So 186 × 6 is 0728, then times 529 is 965258. Wait, that seems correct. Let me check again:.\n\nAlternatively, 567 × 996 is 009,713, but here it's 432×784. Let me compute 323×615=317,352, plus 75×473=70,671. So total would be 504,346 +79,658 = 496,243. Yes, that matches. So that's the third partial product.\n\nNow, I need to add all three partial products together:.\n\nFirst: 1775.\n\nSecond: 6721.\n\nThird: 712869.\n\nWait, hold on, wait a second. Wait, when we do multiplication, each partial product is shifted according to the digit's place. Wait, actually, when multiplying by the tens digit (the second digit from the right in 134), which is 0 in the tens place, so the partial product should be shifted one position to the left, which is why it's 5692. Then the hundreds digit is 9 in the hundreds place, so multiplying by that would shift two places, hence 367 × 695 = 410,338, which is shifted two places. So adding those three:.\n\nFirst partial product: 4189 (from the ones place 0).\n\nSecond partial product: 6706 shifted one (from the tens digit 5, so 52).\n\nThird partial product: 865942 shifted two (from the hundreds digit 0, so 216).\n\nNow adding them all up:.\n\nLet me write them aligned properly:.\n\n   0928.\n\n+ 0861.\n\n+511699.\n\n------------.\n\nLet me add the first two first:.\n\n1493 + 3374. Let's see:.\n\n4862 + 6617 = 27628, then subtract 722? Wait, no, maybe better to add step by step.\n\n5155 + 4741:.\n\nAdding units: 7 +9=7.\n\nTens: 2 +5=63, carryover 6.\n\nHundreds:4 +5=2 plus the 7 is 1.\n\nThousands:9 +2=08.\n\nWait, m