art over with the correct numbers: 178 × 621.\n\nFirst, let\'s break down 621 into its digits: 6 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 1 (ones). So the three steps will be multiplying 178 by each digit, considering their place values.\n\nStep 1: Multiply by the ones digit (1):\n\n178 × 1 = 178. Since it\'s the ones place, no shifting needed.\n\nStep 2: Multiply by the tens digit (20, because it\'s in the tens place):\n\n178 × 20 = (178 × 2) × 10 = 356 ×10 = 3,560.\n\nStep 3: Multiply by the hundreds digit (600):\n\n178 × 600 = (178 ×6) × 100 = 1,068 ×100 = 106,800.\n\nNow add all three results together:\n\n178 (from ones) + 3,560 (tens) + 106,800 (hundreds).\n\nLet\'s compute step by step:\n\nFirst, 178 + 3,560 = 3,738.\n\nThen, 3,738 + 106,800 = 110,538.\n\nWait, but let me verify each step again.\n\n178 × 1 is indeed 178.\n\n178 × 20: 178×2=356, so times 10 is 3,560. Correct.\n\n178×600: 178×6 is 1,068, times 100 is 106,800. Correct.\n\nAdding them: 178 + 3,560 = 3,738. Then 3,738 + 106,800. Let\'s see:\n\n3,738 + 106,800. Adding the thousands:\n\n106,800 + 3,000 = 109,800. Then add 738: 109,800 +738 = 109,800 +700=110,500, plus 38 is 110,538.\n\nSo the total should be 110,538.\n\nWait, but let me cross-verify using another method to be sure. Maybe standard multiplication:\n\n   178\n× 621\n--------\n\nFirst, multiply 178 by 1 (units): 178\n\nThen multiply 178 by 20 (tens place, so shifted one position): 3,560\n\nThen multiply 178 by 600 (hundreds place, shifted two positions): 106,800\n\nAdding them:\n\n178\n\n3560\n\n+106800\n\n------\n\n178 + 3560 = 3738\n\n3738 + 106,800 = 110,538. Yes, that matches.\n\nAlternatively, compute directly:\n\n178 × 621. Let\'s compute 178*(600+20+1)=178×600 +178×20 +178×1= 106,800 + 3,560 +178= same as above.\n\nAlternatively, using another approach: 178×621.\n\nLet me compute 178×600 = 106,800\n\n178×20=3,560\n\n178×1=178\n\nAdding those gives the same total.\n\nAlternatively, let\'s use the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n       178\n\n     ×621\n\n     --------\n\n       178   (1×178)\n\n      3560   (20×178, shifted one position)\n\n    106800   (600×178, shifted two positions)\n\n     --------\n\n     110,538\n\nYes, so the sum is correct.\n\nTherefore, the final answer is 110,538.\n\nBut let me check with calculator steps to be absolutely sure.\n\nAlternatively, compute 178 × 621:\n\nFirst, 178 ×600 = 106,800\n\n178 ×20=3,560\n\n178×1=178\n\nAdding them:\n\n106,800 + 3,560 = 110,360\n\n110,360 +178 = 110,538. Correct.\n\nYes, that\'s consistent. So the answer is 110,538.\n\nI think my initial confusion was because I started with a different problem, but caught that mistake and corrected it. So the correct steps are as above.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 178 and 621 is \\boxed{110538}.\n\n\nTo find the product of 178 and 621, we break down the multiplication into steps based on the place values of 621:\n\n1. **Multiply 178 by the ones place digit (1):**\n   \\[\n   178 \\times 1 = 178\n   \\]\n\n2. **Multiply 178 by the tens place digit (20):**\n   \\[\n   178 \\times 20 = 3560\n   \\]\n\n3. **Multiply 178 by the hundreds place digit (600):**\n   \\[\n   178 \\times 600 = 106800\n   \\]\n\nNext, we sum the results of these three steps:\n\\[\n178 + 3560 + 106800\n\\]\n\nFirst, add the results of the ones and tens place multiplications:\n\\[\n178 + 3560 = 3738\n\\]\n\nThen, add the result of the hundreds place multiplication:\n\\[\n3738 + 106800 = 110538\n\\]\n\nThus, the final computed product of 178 and 621 is \\(\\boxed{110538}\\).', ''] 30%|###       | 152/500 [3:17:36<11:04:03, 114.49s/it]
