 (ones place):\n\n915 × 2 = (900 ×2) + (15 ×2) = 1800 + 30 = 1830.\n\n2. Multiply 915 × 90 (tens place):\n\nFirst compute 915 × 9, then multiply by 10.\n\n915 ×9: Let\'s break it down:\n\n900×9 = 8100,\n\n15×9 = 135,\n\nTotal: 8100 + 135 = 8235.\n\nThen multiply by 10: 8235 ×10 = 82,350.\n\n3. Multiply 915 × 200 (hundreds place):\n\nFirst compute 915 ×2, then multiply by 100.\n\n915 ×2 = 1830,\n\nThen ×100 = 183,000.\n\nNow, sum all three results:\n\nFirst result: 1,830\n\nSecond result: 82,350\n\nThird result: 183,000\n\nAdding them step by step:\n\nStart with 1,830 + 82,350 = 84,180.\n\nThen add 183,000: 84,180 + 183,000 = 267,180.\n\nWait, but let me check this again to ensure accuracy.\n\nWait, 1,830 + 82,350 is indeed 84,180. Then adding 183,000 gives 84,180 + 183,000 = 267,180.\n\nHmm, but let me verify with another method to be sure.\n\nAlternatively, compute 915 ×292:\n\n292 can be written as 300 - 8.\n\nSo, 915 × (300 -8) = 915×300 - 915×8.\n\nCompute each part:\n\n915 ×300 = 915 ×3 ×100 = 2,745 ×100 = 274,500.\n\n915 ×8 = 7,320.\n\nSubtract: 274,500 - 7,320 = 267,180. Same result. So that\'s correct.\n\nTherefore, the product of 915 and 292 is 267,180.\n\nWait, but in my initial reasoning steps, I had messed up the numbers, but after correcting, the answer is 267,180. Let me make sure the steps in the template are filled correctly.\n\nThe user\'s template example had:\n\n# Question:\nWhat is the product of <<number1 such as 27>> and <<number2 such as 153>>?\n# Reasoning:\nLet\'s think step by step. <<number2 153 has three digits, so that we can reason in three steps.>>\n1. <<Multiply number1 27 by the ones place digit 3 of number2 153>>\n2. <<Multiply number1 27 by the tens place digit 50 of number2 153>>\n<<other digits of number2 if it has>>\nAnswer:\nNow, sum all the step results: <<sum of the results>>.\nSo, the final computed product is <<answer>>.\n\nSo applying to the current problem (915 and 292):\n\n1. Multiply 915 by the ones place digit (2) → 91