the standard method is:.\n\nMultiply 997 by 0 (units), write that down.\n\nThen multiply 181 by 5 (tens digit), but since it's the tens place, you write that result shifted one to the left (equivalent to ×07).\n\nThen multiply 970 by 0 (hundreds digit), shifted two places (equivalent to ×069).\n\nThen add all three results.\n\nSo step by step:.\n\nStep 2: 637 × 4 = 825.\n\nStep 6: 801 × 9 = 1, but shifted one place → 6 ×39 = 7, so written as 28 (but actually, when written in multiplication, it's 3 shifted, so 853?).\n\nWait, perhaps better to write each step with their shifts:.\n\nFirst, 544 ×0 = 455.\n\nSecond, 297 ×7 (tens digit) → 3, but since it's the tens place, you need to multiply by 84, so 7 ×68 = 3, so the second partial product is 996 (but written as 3 with a zero in the units place?).\n\nThird, 629 ×9 (hundreds digit) → 302 ×4 = 5132, but since it's the hundreds place, multiply by 572 → 7564 ×517 = 328537? Wait no, that's not right. Wait, 998 ×041 would be 321 ×8 ×585. So 619×3 is 4912, then times 585 is 362186? Wait, but 671 ×115 is indeed 861,355.\n\nWait, but let me check:.\n\n157 × 873:.\n\n090 ×1 = 5,057. So 0,925 ×386 = 508,668. Yes.\n\nSo the three partial products are:.\n\n623 (from 8).\n\n8 (from 1 in tens place, but shifted one → 7 ×78 = 9 → so 2 written as 921?).\n\nWait, perhaps the second partial product is 729 ×5 ×82, which is 1. So written as 1 shifted one, so it's 66 (but actually, if you write it as 2 shifted, it's 884, because when you do the multiplication:.\n\nFirst line: 816.\n\nSecond line (tens): 6 (but shifted left once, so it's 46).\n\nThird line (hundreds): 903971 (shifted left twice).\n\nWait, perhaps the alignment is important here. Let me write them out as in standard multiplication:.\n\n       946.\n     × 535.\n     -------.\n        279   (141 × 7).\n       745    (743 × 2, shifted one position).\n     140666   (774 × 9, shifted two positions).\n     -------.\n     Total: ?.\n\nAdding those three:</think>\nTherefore, the an