×2 =14 +6=20, write 0, carryover 2.\n\n7×3 =21 +2=23. So 2,303. So ×10 is 23,030. Correct.\n\nNow the hundreds digit is 6 (in the hundreds place, so 600):\n\n329 ×600.\n\nCompute 329 ×6 = 1,974, then add two zeros → 197,400.\n\nNow add all three results:\n\n2,961 (from 9) \n\n+23,030 (from 70) \n\n+197,400 (from 600)\n\nAdding first two: 2,961 +23,030 = 26, 961+23,030: 2,961+20,000=22,961, then +3,030=25,991? Wait, let me do it properly:\n\n2,961 +23,030:\n\n2,961 +23,000 =25,961\n\nThen add 30 → 25,991.\n\nThen add 197,400:\n\n25,991 +197,400 = 223,391.\n\nOkay, so that matches the first method. So the answer is 223,391. Wait but the user\'s initial example had a different structure where they broke down the second number\'s digits into hundreds, tens, ones and multiplied step by step. Let me see the original problem\'s example again.\n\nThe user\'s first example was 27 ×153. The reasoning was breaking down 153 into 3, 50, 100, then multiplying each by 27 and adding up. So following that template, for 329 ×679, we should do:\n\nFirst, since 679 has three digits, split into 600,70,9. Then multiply each by 329, then add.\n\nSo step by step:\n\n1. 329 ×9 = 2,961\n\n2. 329 ×70 =23,030\n\n3. 329 ×600=197,400\n\nThen sum all: 2,961 +23,030 +197,400 = 223,391.\n\nYes, so that\'s correct. So the answer is 223,391.\n\nBut in my initial wrong attempt, I had different numbers because I misread the problem as 977×223 and got confused with other numbers. So need to ensure the correct numbers are used here.\n\nWait, the user\'s question is "What is the product of 329 and 679?" So the correct answer is indeed 223,391. Let me verify once more with another method.\n\nAlternatively, using the standard multiplication:\n\n     329\n   × 679\n   -------\n     2961  (329×9)\n    23030  (329×70, shifted one position left)\n   197400  (329×600, shifted two positions left)\n   ---------\n    223391\n\nYes, adding those:\n\n2961 +23030 = 25,991\n\n25,991 +197,400 = 223,391.\n\nAlright, that