final_output:  ['76, 269. Wait, but I\'m confused now. I think I messed up the steps. Let me start over properly.\n\nWait, the user\'s example had 27 × 153, so 153 is 3 ones, 5 tens (so 50), and 1 hundred (100). So they did 27×3, 27×50, 27×100, then added them. So following that structure for 215 × 452:\n\nFirst, 452 is 2 ones, 5 tens (50), 4 hundreds (400). So:\n\n1. 215 × 2 = 430.\n\n2. 215 × 50 = 10,750.\n\n3. 215 × 400 = 86,000.\n\nThen add them all: 430 + 10,750 = 11,180; then +86,000 = 97,180. So the answer is 97,180.\n\nWait, but the user\'s original problem was 651 × 318. Wait, in the problem given by the user now, it\'s "What is the product of 651 and 318?" So the numbers are 651 and 318. So I need to adjust my steps for those numbers.\n\nLet me correct that. Let\'s start fresh with the actual problem: 651 × 318.\n\nSo breaking down 318 into its place values:\n\n3 (hundreds), 1 (tens), 8 (ones). So:\n\n1. Multiply 651 by 8 (ones place): 651 ×8.\n\nLet me compute that: 600×8=4800, 50×8=400, 1×8=8. Total: 4800+400=5200, +8=5208.\n\n2. Multiply 651 by 10 (the tens place digit is 1, so 1×10=10). So 651×10=6510.\n\nBut wait, actually, it\'s 651 × 1 (the digit) ×10, so 651×10. That\'s straightforward.\n\n3. Multiply 651 by 300 (the hundreds place digit is 3, so 3×100=300). So 651×300.\n\nCalculating that: 651×3=1953, then add two zeros: 195,300.\n\nNow, sum all three results: 5208 + 6510 + 195,300.\n\nFirst add 5208 and 6510: 5208+6510. Let\'s see, 5000+6000=11,000; 208+510=718. So total is 11,000+718=11,718.\n\nThen add that to 195,300: 195,300 +11,718.\n\n195,300 +10,000 = 205,300; then +1,718 gives 207,018.\n\nSo the final product is 207,018.\n\nWait, let me check with another method to be sure.\n\nAlternatively, 651×318.\n\nWe can use standard multiplication:\n\n    651\n× 318\n--------\nFirst multiply by 8:\n\n651×8 = 5208\n\nThen multiply by 10 (the 1 in tens place):\n\n651×10 = 6510\n\nThen multiply by 300 (the 3 in hundreds place):\n\n651×300 = 195,300\n\nAdding them: 5208 +6510 = 11,718; 11,718 +195,300 = 207,018. Same result.\n\nAlternatively, using another approach, (650+1)(300+18) but that might complicate. Alternatively, verify with calculator steps:\n\n651×318:\n\n651×300 = 195,300.\n\n651×18 = 651×10 +651×8 =6,510 +5,208 =11,718.\n\nTotal: 195,300 +11,718=207,018. Yep, matches.\n\nSo the answer is correct.\n\nBut let me also check the steps in the reasoning as per the template. The user wants the reasoning to show each step for each digit, so let\'s structure it properly as per the example.\n\nOriginal example had:\n\n1. Multiply number1 by the ones digit (3), so 27×3=81\n\n2. Multiply by the tens digit (50), so 27×50=1350\n\n3. Multiply by the hundreds digit (100), so 27×100=2700\n\nThen sum 81+1350+2700=4131.\n\nSo following that structure for the problem 651×318:\n\nNumber2 is 318, which has digits 3 (hundreds),1 (tens),8 (ones).\n\nSo:\n\n1. Multiply 651 by 8 (ones place): 651×8=5208\n\n2. Multiply 651 by 10 (the tens digit is 1, so 1×10=10): 651×10=6510\n\n3. Multiply 651 by 300 (the hundreds digit 3×100=300): 651×300=195,300\n\nSum all three: 5208+6510=11,718; 11,718+195,300=207,018.\n\nSo the answer is 207,018.\n\nI think that\'s correct. Let me just check once more with another method. For instance, using the distributive property:\n\n651×318 = 651×(300+18) = 651×300 +651×18 =195,300 + (651×10 +651×8)= same as before. So yes.\n\nTherefore, the reasoning is correct.\n\n\n\n# Question:\nWhat is the product of 651 and 318?\n# Reasoning:\nLet\'s think step by step. 318 has three digits, so that we can reason in three steps.\n1. Multiply 651 by the ones place digit 8 of 318:  \n   \\(651 \\times 8 = 5,\\!208\\).\n2. Multiply 651 by the tens place digit 10 (since the tens digit is 1):  \n   \\(651 \\times 10 = 6,\\!510\\).\n3. Multiply 651 by the hundreds place digit 300 (since the hundreds digit is 3):  \n   \\(651 \\times 300 = 195,\\!300\\).\n\nNow, sum all the step results:  \n\\(5,\\!208 + 6,\\!510 = 1