irst step: 0 × 850 = 0.\n\nSecond step: 3 (in the tens place, so 30) ×850. So 3×850=2,550, then shifted one position to the left (because it's the tens place) → 25,500.\n\nThird step: 1 (in the hundreds place, so 100) ×850 = 85,000, shifted two positions left → which it already is.\n\nAdding those together: 0 + 25,500 + 85,000 = 110,500. Same result.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the user wants the step-by-step breakdown as per the template, so let's structure it that way.\n\nThe problem is 850 ×130.\n\nFollowing the template's reasoning style:\n\nFirst, since 130 is a three-digit number, we can break it down into 100 + 30 + 0. But since the last digit is 0, that step might be trivial.\n\nBut following the example given in the initial template:\n\nOriginal example was 27 ×153:\n\n1. Multiply 27 by 3 (ones place),\n\n2. Multiply 27 by 50 (tens place),\n\n3. Multiply 27 by 100 (hundreds place),\n\nThen add all three.\n\nSo for 850 ×130:\n\nNumber2 is 130, which has three digits: 1 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 0 (ones).\n\nThus:\n\n1. Multiply 850 by 0 (ones place digit) → 0.\n\n2. Multiply 850 by 30 (tens place digit is 3, so 3×10=30).\n\n3. Multiply 850 by 100 (hundreds place digit is 1, so 1×100=100).\n\nThen sum those three results.\n\nCalculating each step:\n\n1. 850 ×0 = 0.\n\n2. 850 ×30: 850 ×3 = 2,550; then ×10 = 25,500.\n\n3. 850 ×100 = 85,000.\n\nAdding them: 0 +25,500 +85,000 = 110,500.\n\nThus, the answer is 110,500.\n\nI think that's correct. Let me check with another method: 850 ×130.\n\nAlternatively, 850 ×130 is equal to 85 ×10 ×13 ×10 = (85×13) ×100, which we did earlier as 1,105 ×100=110,500. So that confirms it.\n\nTherefore, the final answer should be 110,500.\n\nJust making sure I didn't make any arithmetic errors. Let me re-calculate 85 ×13:\n\n85 ×10 = 850,\n\n85 ×3 = 255,\n\n850 +255 = 1,105. Yes.\n\nThen ×100 is 110,500. Correct.\n\nAnother way: 850 ×100 = 85,000,\n\n850 ×30 = 25,500,\n\nAdding those gives 85k +25.5k = 110.5k, so 110,500. Yep.\n\nAlright, looks solid.\n\n\n\n# Question:\nWhat is the product of 850 and 130?\n# Reasoning:\nLet's think step by step. 130 has three digits, so that we can reason in three steps.\n1. Multiply 850 by the ones place digit 0 of 130:  \n   \\(850 \\times 0 = 0\\).\n2. Multiply 850 by the tens place digit 30 of 130:  \n   \\(850 \\times 30 = 25,\\!500\\).\n3. Multiply 850 by the hundreds place digit 100 of 130:  \n   \\(850 \\times 100 = 85,\\!000\\).\n\nAnswer:  \nNow, sum all the step results: \\(0 + 25,\\!500 + 85,\\!000 = 110,\\!500\\).  \nSo, the final computed product is \\boxed{110500}.", ''] 99%|#########8| 493/500 [9:35:10<05:52, 50.31s/it]
